*               text, work in progress, the thing in focus), whatever the theme.  In mono no role uses dim (SD's xterm entry has none); the six looks are different from
*               one another, and the words on the screen carry the meaning.
* 1 Nov 26 dm  S.99: the op 'syntax' (the nine syntax styles of the dark or the light kind put on the theme in force, nothing else changed), so that SED can be in the
*               site's theme (the pages' colours) and still have SYNTAX LIGHT.  Every other op is as it was.
* 8 Nov 26 dm  S.172: two near-monochrome themes, paper (black and dark blue on light grey; the shipped SCREENTHEME) and night (white and yellow on black), the owner
*               having found blue's menu bar (black and red on cyan) and its text hard to read.  Every style of both has an explicit background (a style with only a
*               foreground would show the terminal's own background inside a light window), the mnemonic letters are underlined as well as coloured, and the
*               syntax styles have their own set for each.  The menu bar and the menus are the inverse of the page.  Nothing on the light grey is bold (a
*               terminal makes bold text bright, and bright black or blue is pale on grey), and the four raw styles take the theme's two colours, as two
*               dozen pages draw their lists in normal.  The list is blue classic dark light mono night paper.  A readability review (Fable 5.1, WCAG 2
*               ratios in the xterm and GNOME Tango palettes) found nothing below 3:1; below 4.5 only blue (Tango 4.06), red (4.03) and magenta (xterm
*               3.72) on the light grey - every colour but black is between 3 and 4.5 there without bold, the cost of syntax colour in a light 8-colour
*               theme - and paper's focus and error rely on bold being made bright for 4.5.
* 8 Nov 26 dm  S.179: the person's own theme.  The ops 'site' and 'save'; 'default' takes 'site'.  Kept inside SD (the owner: "Inside SD (tighter)"),
*               as the record theme.<login name> of &menus& in the session's account - the Recent group's file - so no OS gate is crossed.
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.186 (Fable's review): mono's focus is reverse, bold and underline (it was reverse, which is highlight's and the bar's look, so on a terminal
*               with no colour the focused box looked like the selected row); paper's focus is underlined as well as bold, so it does not rely on bold being bright.
* 10 Nov 26 dm  S.199 (owner: "three themes, dark, light and mono are exactly the same"; "Yes, drop them"; "there is no need to keep them for programs"): the themes dark and
*               light are gone.  The syntax palettes of those names stay: classic and blue use the dark one, and an editor's SYNTAX LIGHT the light one.
* 10 Nov 26 dm  S.173: hotkey.sel in every theme; paper's and night's selected entry is white on blue, no longer the page's colours (it looked like a hole in the open menu).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_theme(op, a, b, c, d, ret)
*
*   The library draws with STYLES.  Every cell of the shadow keeps the one-character CODE of its style
*   (sl.h) and a THEME says what each code looks like on the terminal: a colour for the text, a colour
*   for the background and any of bold, dim, reverse and underline.  The codes do not depend on the
*   theme, so one can be switched while there is something on the screen and the screen then repainted.
*   The default theme, mono, uses only reverse video and underline and reads on any terminal; it is what
*   a program gets until it asks for another, and what SED always uses.
*
*     style       code  mono looks like       what it is for
*     normal      n     the terminal's own    the program's own output
*     reverse     r     reverse video         (raw: the same in every theme but paper and night, which give the four the theme's two colours)
*     underline   u     underline             (raw)
*     both        x     reverse and underline (raw)
*     bold        b     bold                  bold text on the desktop: it keeps the desktop's colours (a theme may change it)
*     window      w     normal                the body of a dialogue or a form, and what is drawn in it
*     highlight   h     reverse               the selected entry, the button or field that has the focus
*     bar         a     reverse               a menu bar and the body of a menu
*     barsel      s     normal                the selected entry of a bar
*     hotkey      k     underline             a mnemonic letter
*     hotkey.bar  j     reverse and underline a mnemonic letter on a bar
*     hotkey.sel  Z     underline             a mnemonic letter on the selected entry of a bar
*     field       e     underline             an entry field that has not the focus
*     disabled    d     normal                an entry that cannot be chosen
*     desktop     y     normal                what SCREEN CLEAR, TEXT and BOX draw on: the background
*     text        t     normal                the text of a memo (a text area), a viewer or a table
*     header      g     underline and bold    the heading row of a table
*
*   The roles (29 Oct 26).  Each has a look in every theme; the colour ones carry both colours (a style replaces the whole attribute set):
*     error       f     reverse and bold      something failed or was refused (also the syntax colour of an error)
*     warning     U     bold and underline    something to take care over
*     success     V     bold                  something that worked
*     readonly    W     underline             text that cannot be changed
*     busy        X     reverse and underline work in progress
*     focus       Y     reverse, bold, under  the thing that has the keyboard, where highlight is not enough (9 Nov 26: it was reverse, the same as highlight)
*
*   op   'code'   a = a style name, or a code itself; ret = the code, n for a name it does not know
*         'set'    a = the name of a theme: mono, classic, blue, night or paper.  ret 0 done; 1 refused, because the
*                  terminal has no colour (or there is no terminal) and the theme needs it - the theme in
*                  force is left as it was; 2 no theme of that name.  a = '' builds the sequences again for
*                  the terminal type now in force.
*         'style'  a = a style name, b = foreground, c = background, d = flags: changes that style of the
*                  theme in force.  ret 0 done; 2 the style is not one a theme may change (the raw ones, or
*                  a name that is not a style); 3 a colour or flag it does not know.  A colour is black,
*                  blue, green, cyan, red, magenta, brown (or yellow), white, or empty for the terminal's
*                  own; the flags are any of bold, dim, reverse and underline, separated by spaces or +.
*         'name'   ret = the name of the theme in force
*         'list'   ret = the names of the themes, one to a field, in the one order every program shows them: blue, classic, mono, night, paper
*         'next'   a = the name of a theme ('' for the one in force); ret = the next one in that order that THIS terminal can show, wrapping.  A
*                  terminal with no colour (or no terminal) can show mono only, so ret is mono whatever a was.
*         'site'   ret = the theme a program starts in: the person's own (op save) when they kept one in this account, else the one SCREENTHEME
*                  names in sd.conf (lower case; '' when it names none).  Programs that choose their theme themselves (the IDE, SED, the
*                  debugger, SP.VIEW) ask this rather than reading SCREENTHEME, so that all of them start where the pages do.
*         'save'   a = a theme the person chose (Ctrl-T, View > Theme): kept as the record theme.<login name> of &menus& in the account the
*                  session is in (made when it is not there), and taken by 'default' and 'site' from then on.  Nothing outside the account is
*                  touched.  ret 0 kept; 1 not kept (the file could not be made or written: the theme lasts for this session); 2 not a theme.
*         'default' (SCREEN OPEN) takes the theme 'site' names, unless the program asked for one; a blank or unknown name, or a
*                  theme this terminal cannot show, leaves the library as it is (mono).
*         'syntax'  a = dark or light: the nine syntax styles (comment string constant keyword type preproc special error todo) of that kind are put on the
*                  theme in force and nothing else changes (1 Nov 26, S.99: SED is in the site's blue theme with the light syntax colours when the person asks for
*                  SYNTAX LIGHT).  ret 0 done; 2 for a kind it does not know.  The theme in force is mono until something set one: it is read first.
*
*   $sl_sgr turns a code into what to DISPLAY to put the terminal into that style: it reads the sequences
*   this routine keeps in the common block /slthm/.  A sequence starts by switching every attribute off,
*   so it does not depend on the style before it.
*   Colour comes from the terminal's own capabilities (SD's @(-37, n) and @(-38, n)), which need a
*   terminfo entry with setf and setb; there are eight colours, and bold makes the text bright.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_theme(op, a, b, c, d, ret)
$catalog $sl_theme

$include sl.h

   common /slthm/ th.name, th.codes, th.defs, th.seqs, th.names, th.asked

   * A common block's variables start as 0 (measured 12 Oct 26: they are not unassigned), so the test is on the value
   fresh = 0
   if unassigned(th.codes) then
      fresh = 1
   end else
      if th.codes # SL.CODES then fresh = 1
   end
   if fresh then
      th.name = ''
      th.codes = SL.CODES
      th.defs = ''
      th.seqs = ''
      * The names of the styles, in the order of the codes
      * (field marks: LOCATE x IN list<1> searches fields; a list of values needs list<1,1>)
      th.names = 'normal' : @fm : 'reverse' : @fm : 'underline' : @fm : 'both' : @fm : 'bold' : @fm : 'window'
      th.names := @fm : 'highlight' : @fm : 'bar' : @fm : 'barsel' : @fm : 'hotkey' : @fm : 'hotkey.bar'
      th.names := @fm : 'field' : @fm : 'disabled' : @fm : 'desktop' : @fm : 'text' : @fm : 'header'
      th.names := @fm : 'comment' : @fm : 'string' : @fm : 'constant' : @fm : 'keyword' : @fm : 'type'
      th.names := @fm : 'preproc' : @fm : 'special' : @fm : 'error' : @fm : 'todo' : @fm : 'bar.disabled'
      th.names := @fm : 'gutter'                    ;* 14 Oct 26: the line numbers of a text area (code G: every letter was taken)
      th.names := @fm : 'pane'                      ;* 14 Oct 26: the body of a tree, so that it can differ from the window's frame (code P)
      th.names := @fm : 'tab' : @fm : 'scroll'      ;* 14 Oct 26: the strip of tabs (code T) and the scroll bar column of a list, text area, table or tree (code B)
      th.names := @fm : 'warning' : @fm : 'success' : @fm : 'readonly'     ;* 29 Oct 26: the roles (codes U V W), before the user styles
      th.names := @fm : 'busy' : @fm : 'focus'                             ;* (codes X Y)
      th.names := @fm : 'hotkey.sel'                                       ;* 10 Nov 26 (S.173, code Z)
      for q = 1 to 16
         th.names := @fm : 'user' : q                 ;* 22 Oct 26: the spare styles a control's COLOR clause takes (codes A C D E F H I J K L M N O Q R S)
      next q
   end
   names = th.names

   ret = ''
   begin case
      case op = 'code'
         ret = 'n'
         s = downcase(a)
         locate s in names<1> setting i then
            ret = th.codes[i, 1]
         end else
            if len(a) = 1 and index(th.codes, a, 1) then ret = a
         end

      case op = 'name'
         ret = th.name
         if ret = '' then ret = 'mono'

      case op = 'list'
         gosub theme.order
         ret = thorder

      case op = 'next'
         gosub next.theme

      case op = 'set'
         gosub set.theme
         if b = 'asked' and ret = 0 then th.asked = 1      ;* a program asked for this theme: the site's default is not for it

      case op = 'style'
         gosub set.style
         if ret = 0 then th.asked = 1

      case op = 'slot'
         * A control asks for its own colours (the COLOR clause): a = foreground, b = background, c = flags (bold dim reverse underline).  ret is the code of a
         * USER style (user1 to user16) that holds exactly those, one already given to the same colours or the first free one; 'none' when the colours cannot
         * be shown (a phantom, a terminal with no colour: the clause is then ignored); 'full' when all sixteen are in use; 'bad' for a colour or a flag
         * it does not know.  A theme leaves the user styles as they are, so a colour survives SCREEN THEME; SCREEN CLOSE empties them.
         gosub get.slot

      case op = 'syntax'
         gosub set.syntax

      case op = 'site'
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.179): the theme a program starts in - the person's own choice when they made one, else the site's
         gosub site.theme
         ret = st.name

      case op = 'save'
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.179): keep a = the theme this person chose (Ctrl-T, View > Theme) for their next sessions in this account
         gosub save.theme

      case op = 'default'
         * SCREEN OPEN: the person's own theme (op save), else the theme the administrator chose for the site (SCREENTHEME in
         * sd.conf), unless the program has asked for one itself.  A name that is no theme, or a theme this terminal cannot
         * show (no colour), is not an error here: the library stays as it is.
         if not(th.asked) then
            gosub site.theme
            dn = st.name
            if dn # '' and dn # downcase(th.name) then
               a = dn
               gosub set.theme
            end
         end

      case op = 'reset'
         * SCREEN CLOSE, and the clean-up after a program that did not: the next program starts from mono, having asked for nothing
         if not(unassigned(th.defs)) then
            ufirst = len(th.codes) - 15
            for s = ufirst to len(th.codes)
               th.defs<s> = ''                          ;* the user styles go with the program that asked for them
            next s
         end
         a = 'mono'
         gosub set.theme
         th.asked = 0
   end case

   return

* The theme the caller named (or, for null, the one in force) takes over
set.theme:
   nm = downcase(a)
   if nm = '' then
      nm = th.name
      if nm = '' then nm = 'mono'
   end
   spec = ''
   begin case
      case nm = 'mono'
         spec<-1> = 'highlight=,,reverse'
         spec<-1> = 'bar=,,reverse'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey=,,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.bar=,,reverse underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.sel=,,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'field=,,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'header=,,underline bold'
         spec<-1> = 'bar.disabled=,,reverse dim'   ;* 13 Oct 26: a menu item that cannot be chosen
         gosub roles.mono
      case nm = 'classic'
         spec<-1> = 'desktop=white,blue,'
         spec<-1> = 'bold=white,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'window=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'highlight=white,green,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'bar=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'barsel=white,green,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey=red,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.bar=red,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.sel=red,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'field=white,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'disabled=black,white,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'text=white,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'header=black,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'bar.disabled=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'gutter=cyan,blue,'
         spec<-1> = 'pane=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'tab=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'scroll=black,white,bold'     ;* 22 Oct 26: dark grey, as in blue
         spec<-1> = 'warning=black,brown,'        ;* 29 Oct 26: the roles carry both colours, a style replaces the whole attribute set
         spec<-1> = 'success=black,green,'
         spec<-1> = 'readonly=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'busy=white,magenta,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'focus=white,cyan,bold'
         gosub syntax.dark
      case nm = 'blue'
         spec<-1> = 'window=white,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'highlight=black,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'bar=black,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'barsel=white,black,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey=brown,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.bar=red,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.sel=brown,blue,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'field=black,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'disabled=cyan,blue,'
         spec<-1> = 'text=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'header=brown,black,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'bar.disabled=blue,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'gutter=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'pane=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'tab=black,cyan,'
         spec<-1> = 'scroll=black,cyan,bold'      ;* 22 Oct 26: dark grey (bold black), not black - the thumb was the colour of the black text area beside it
         spec<-1> = 'warning=black,brown,'        ;* 29 Oct 26: the roles carry both colours, a style replaces the whole attribute set
         spec<-1> = 'success=black,green,'
         spec<-1> = 'readonly=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'busy=white,magenta,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'focus=white,cyan,bold'
         gosub syntax.dark
      case nm = 'paper'
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.172): near-monochrome, dark text on light grey (white without bold is light grey on the eight-colour terminals); what is selected is bright
         * white on black, a mnemonic letter dark blue and underlined.  The shipped SCREENTHEME.  NO BOLD ON THE LIGHT BACKGROUND: most terminals make bold text
         * bright, and bright black or bright blue on light grey is the pale text the owner could not read (measured in the first capture: grey and light blue).
         * The four raw styles take the theme's two colours here, unlike every other theme: two dozen pages draw their lists in normal, which would otherwise show
         * the terminal's own colours inside the grey page; reverse is still reverse (white on black), underline still underline.
         spec<-1> = 'normal=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'reverse=black,white,reverse'
         spec<-1> = 'underline=black,white,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'both=black,white,reverse underline'
         spec<-1> = 'desktop=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'bold=black,white,underline'        ;* not blue: blue on grey is disabled and read-only only (readability review, Fable 5.1)
         spec<-1> = 'window=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'highlight=white,black,bold'
         * the menu bar and the menus are the INVERSE of the page (light grey on black, the letter yellow): a menu the same colours as the page under it had no edge
         spec<-1> = 'bar=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'barsel=white,blue,'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey=blue,white,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.bar=brown,black,bold underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.sel=brown,blue,bold underline'
         spec<-1> = 'field=black,white,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'disabled=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'text=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'header=black,white,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'bar.disabled=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'gutter=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'pane=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'tab=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'scroll=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'warning=black,brown,'
         spec<-1> = 'success=black,green,'
         spec<-1> = 'readonly=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'busy=white,magenta,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'focus=white,blue,bold underline'
         gosub syntax.paper
      case nm = 'night'
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.172): the dark kind of paper, white on black with yellow (bold brown) for what stands out; the bar and the menus are the inverse of the
         * page, black on light grey, as paper's are; like paper no style on the light grey is bold; the four raw styles take the theme's colours
         spec<-1> = 'normal=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'reverse=white,black,reverse'
         spec<-1> = 'underline=white,black,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'both=white,black,reverse underline'
         spec<-1> = 'desktop=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'bold=brown,black,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'window=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'highlight=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'bar=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'barsel=white,blue,'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey=brown,black,bold underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.bar=blue,white,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'hotkey.sel=brown,blue,bold underline'
         spec<-1> = 'field=white,black,underline'
         spec<-1> = 'disabled=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'text=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'header=brown,black,bold underline'
         spec<-1> = 'bar.disabled=blue,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'gutter=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'pane=white,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'tab=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'scroll=black,white,'
         spec<-1> = 'warning=black,brown,'
         spec<-1> = 'success=black,green,'
         spec<-1> = 'readonly=cyan,black,'
         spec<-1> = 'busy=white,magenta,bold'
         spec<-1> = 'focus=black,white,'
         gosub syntax.night
      case 1
         ret = 2
         return
   end case

   * A colour theme needs a terminal that can show colour
   if nm # 'mono' then
      if system(25) then
         ret = 1
         return
      end
      if @(-37, 7) = '' or @(-38, 0) = '' then
         ret = 1
         return
      end
   end

   ufirst = len(th.codes) - 15                     ;* the user styles (COLOR clauses) are the program's, not the theme's: they are kept across the change
   keepu = ''
   for s = ufirst to len(th.codes)
      keepu<s> = th.defs<s>
   next s
   gosub clear.defs
   n = dcount(spec, @fm)
   for k = 1 to n
      line = spec<k>
      sname = field(line, '=', 1)
      rest = field(line, '=', 2)
      locate sname in names<1> setting i then
         sfg = field(rest, ',', 1)
         sbg = field(rest, ',', 2)
         sfl = field(rest, ',', 3)
         gosub put.def
      end
   next k
   for s = ufirst to len(th.codes)
      if keepu<s> # '' then th.defs<s> = keepu<s>
   next s
   gosub build.seqs
   th.name = nm
   ret = 0
   return

* The syntax styles of the kind a on the theme in force (the op 'syntax'): the same nine lines the themes take, put through put.def, and the sequences made again.  ret 0, or 2 for a kind it does not know
set.syntax:
   ret = 2
   spec = ''
   begin case
      case downcase(a) = 'dark'
         gosub syntax.dark
      case downcase(a) = 'light'
         gosub syntax.light
      case 1
         return
   end case
   if th.name = '' then gosub load.mono
   n = dcount(spec, @fm)
   for k = 1 to n
      line = spec<k>
      sname = field(line, '=', 1)
      rest = field(line, '=', 2)
      locate sname in names<1> setting i then
         sfg = field(rest, ',', 1)
         sbg = field(rest, ',', 2)
         sfl = field(rest, ',', 3)
         gosub put.def
      end
   next k
   gosub build.seqs
   ret = 0
   return

* The syntax styles (what $sl_syntax gives the editor): foreground colours only, on the terminal's own background
syntax.dark:
   spec<-1> = 'comment=blue,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'string=green,,'
   spec<-1> = 'constant=magenta,,'
   spec<-1> = 'keyword=brown,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'type=cyan,,'
   spec<-1> = 'preproc=red,,'
   spec<-1> = 'special=cyan,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'error=white,red,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'todo=black,brown,'
   return

syntax.light:
   spec<-1> = 'comment=green,,'
   spec<-1> = 'string=red,,'
   spec<-1> = 'constant=magenta,,'
   spec<-1> = 'keyword=blue,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'type=blue,,'
   spec<-1> = 'preproc=magenta,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'special=red,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'error=white,red,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'todo=black,brown,'
   return

* 8 Nov 26 (S.172): the syntax styles of paper and night carry the theme's background, so that a coloured word sits in the same light (or black) window as the rest
syntax.paper:
   spec<-1> = 'comment=magenta,white,'
   spec<-1> = 'string=red,white,'
   spec<-1> = 'constant=red,white,'
   spec<-1> = 'keyword=blue,white,'
   spec<-1> = 'type=blue,white,underline'
   spec<-1> = 'preproc=magenta,white,underline'
   spec<-1> = 'special=black,white,underline'
   spec<-1> = 'error=white,red,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'todo=black,brown,'
   return

syntax.night:
   spec<-1> = 'comment=cyan,black,'
   spec<-1> = 'string=green,black,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'constant=green,black,'                 ;* bold magenta is 3.2:1 where bold is not made bright (GNOME's default); green is 5.9 or more
   spec<-1> = 'keyword=brown,black,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'type=cyan,black,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'preproc=red,black,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'special=white,black,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'error=white,red,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'todo=black,brown,'
   return

* The roles of 29 Oct 26 (S.88 3.7a).  Six looks of flags only in mono (no dim: the xterm entry has none), pairwise different; a colour theme keeps the flags where
* the colour is only a foreground, so the role still reads on a terminal that loses the colour
roles.mono:
   spec<-1> = 'error=,,reverse bold'
   spec<-1> = 'warning=,,bold underline'
   spec<-1> = 'success=,,bold'
   spec<-1> = 'readonly=,,underline'
   spec<-1> = 'busy=,,reverse underline'
   spec<-1> = 'focus=,,reverse bold underline'     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.186): plain reverse was highlight's and the bar's look; all three is no other style's
   return

* The one order the themes are shown in
theme.order:
   thorder = 'blue' : @fm : 'classic' : @fm : 'mono' : @fm : 'night' : @fm : 'paper'
   return

* 8 Nov 26 (S.179) - the person's own theme, kept inside SD (the owner's choice, 8 Nov: "Inside SD (tighter)"), the way the home screen keeps
* its Recent group: the record theme.<login name> of the file &menus& in the account the session is in.  Nothing is read or written
* outside the account, so no gate is crossed.  What is kept is a NAME, accepted only when it is one of the themes; anything else in the
* record is ignored.  st.name = that name, else the site's SCREENTHEME (lower case, trimmed; '' when the site names none).
site.theme:
   st.name = ''
   gosub theme.record.id
   open '&menus&' to st.f then
      read st.rec from st.f, st.id then
         st.want = downcase(trim(st.rec<1>))
         gosub theme.order
         locate st.want in thorder<1> setting st.i then st.name = st.want
      end
      close st.f
   end
   if st.name = '' then st.name = downcase(trim(config('SCREENTHEME')))
   return

* a = the theme to keep.  ret 0 kept; 1 it could not be kept (the file could not be made or written: it lasts for this session only);
* 2 a is not a theme.  The file &menus& is made in the account when it is not there, as MY.MENU and the Recent group make it.
save.theme:
   ret = 2
   sv.name = downcase(trim(a))
   gosub theme.order
   locate sv.name in thorder<1> setting sv.i else return
   ret = 1
   gosub theme.record.id
   open '&menus&' to sv.f else
      execute 'create.file &menus& dynamic' capturing sv.out
      open '&menus&' to sv.f else return
   end
   sv.ok = @true
   write sv.name to sv.f, st.id on error sv.ok = @false
   close sv.f
   if sv.ok then ret = 0
   return

theme.record.id:
   st.id = 'theme.' : downcase(@logname)
   return

* ret = the theme after a (or after the one in force) that this terminal can show; mono alone when it has no colour
next.theme:
   gosub theme.order
   ret = 'mono'
   nxcur = downcase(trim(a))
   if nxcur = '' then nxcur = th.name
   if nxcur = '' then nxcur = 'mono'
   nxok = 1
   if system(25) then nxok = 0
   if nxok then
      if @(-37, 7) = '' or @(-38, 0) = '' then nxok = 0
   end
   if nxok = 0 then return
   nxpos = 0
   locate nxcur in thorder<1> setting nxi then
      nxpos = nxi
   end else
      nxpos = 0
   end
   nxpos = nxpos + 1
   if nxpos > dcount(thorder, @fm) then nxpos = 1
   ret = thorder<nxpos>
   return

* The theme mono, before anything has been asked for (the first drawing); the caller's ret is kept
load.mono:
   save.a = a
   save.ret = ret
   a = 'mono'
   gosub set.theme
   a = save.a
   ret = save.ret
   return

* Every style plain, then reverse, underline and both (raw) and bold (a theme may colour it)
clear.defs:
   th.defs = ''
   n = len(th.codes)
   for i = 1 to n
      th.defs<i> = '' : @vm : '' : @vm : ''
   next i
   th.defs<2> = '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'reverse'
   th.defs<3> = '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'underline'
   th.defs<4> = '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'reverse underline'
   th.defs<5> = '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'bold'
   return

* One style of the theme in force
set.style:
   s = downcase(a)
   locate s in names<1> setting i else
      ret = 2
      return
   end
   if i <= 4 then
      ret = 2                                ;* normal and the three raw styles are the same in every theme
      return
   end
   sfg = b
   sbg = c
   sfl = d
   if th.name = '' then gosub load.mono
   gosub put.def
   if ret = 0 then gosub build.seqs
   return

* A user style for the colours a (foreground), b (background) and the flags c: the one that already holds them (value 4 of its definition is the request, in
* lower case), else the first free one.  ret is its code, or none, full or bad (see the slot op)
get.slot:
   ret = 'bad'
   if th.name = '' then gosub load.mono
   sfg = a
   sbg = b
   sfl = c
   if trim(sfg) # '' or trim(sbg) # '' then
      if system(25) then
         ret = 'none'
         return
      end
      if @(-37, 7) = '' or @(-38, 0) = '' then
         ret = 'none'
         return
      end
   end
   skey = downcase(trim(sfg)) : '|' : downcase(trim(sbg)) : '|' : downcase(trim(convert('+,', '  ', sfl)))
   ufirst = len(th.codes) - 15
   have = 0
   free = 0
   for s = ufirst to len(th.codes)
      if th.defs<s, 4> = skey and have = 0 then have = s
      if th.defs<s, 4> = '' and free = 0 then free = s
   next s
   begin case
      case have > 0
         ret = th.codes[have, 1]
      case free = 0
         ret = 'full'
      case 1
         i = free
         gosub put.def
         if ret = 0 then
            th.defs<i, 4> = skey
            ucode = th.codes[i, 1]                    ;* before build.seqs, which loops on i
            gosub build.seqs
            ret = ucode
         end else
            ret = 'bad'
         end
   end case
   return

* Style number i takes the colours sfg on sbg and the flags sfl; ret 0, or 3 for something it does not know
put.def:
   ret = 3
   fg = sfg
   gosub colour.number
   if cn = -2 then return
   fnum = cn
   fg = sbg
   gosub colour.number
   if cn = -2 then return
   bnum = cn
   fl = trim(convert('+,', '  ', downcase(sfl)))
   nf = dcount(fl, ' ')
   if fl = '' then nf = 0
   for kf = 1 to nf                                  ;* (not k: the caller is looping on it)
      word = field(fl, ' ', kf)
      if word # 'bold' and word # 'dim' and word # 'reverse' and word # 'underline' then return
   next kf
   fnum = if fnum < 0 then '' else fnum
   bnum = if bnum < 0 then '' else bnum
   th.defs<i> = fnum : @vm : bnum : @vm : fl
   ret = 0
   return

* fg = a colour name; cn = its number in the order of AT.BLACK ... AT.WHITE, -1 for none, -2 if it is not a colour
colour.number:
   cn = -2
   cname = downcase(trim(fg))
   begin case
      case cname = '' or cname = 'default' ; cn = -1
      case cname = 'black' ; cn = 0
      case cname = 'blue' ; cn = 1
      case cname = 'green' ; cn = 2
      case cname = 'cyan' ; cn = 3
      case cname = 'red' ; cn = 4
      case cname = 'magenta' ; cn = 5
      case cname = 'brown' or cname = 'yellow' ; cn = 6
      case cname = 'white' ; cn = 7
   end case
   return

* The sequence for every code, from the definitions
build.seqs:
   th.seqs = ''
   n = len(th.codes)
   for i = 1 to n
      dd = th.defs<i>
      sq = @(-14) : @(-16)
      fl = dd<1, 3>
      if index(fl, 'reverse', 1) then sq := @(-13)
      if index(fl, 'underline', 1) then sq := @(-15)
      if index(fl, 'bold', 1) then sq := @(-58)
      if index(fl, 'dim', 1) then sq := @(-11)
      if dd<1, 1> # '' then sq := @(-37, dd<1, 1>)
      if dd<1, 2> # '' then sq := @(-38, dd<1, 2>)
      th.seqs<i> = sq
   next i
   return

end

